WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue {
    color: white;
    background-color: #383943A3;
}

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.559
At its core, AI is about building software applications
that, at least in some aspects, mimic human behaviors

00:00:07.559 --> 00:00:08.622
and capabilities.

00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:12.483
So let's start with that most human of activities,
having a chat.

00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:21.162
Here's a chat application that I can use to find information
about the history of computing, such as, who was Ada

00:00:21.162 --> 00:00:21.945
Lovelace?

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:26.807
And I get a comprehensive and relevant response.

00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:31.829
Okay, let's continue the conversation and find out
more about her work with Charles Babbage.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:42.047
And the model understands the context of a follow-up
prompt and responds with more information.

00:00:43.848 --> 00:00:45.926
So, how does generative AI work?

00:00:46.326 --> 00:00:47.556
Let's take a closer look.

00:00:48.756 --> 00:00:54.595
We start by collecting a large volume of text; which
we'll break down into individual text tokens and use

00:00:54.595 --> 00:00:56.325
to train a large language model.

00:00:56.475 --> 00:01:00.434
The model encapsulates the semantic relationships
between the tokens.

00:01:00.684 --> 00:01:06.524
So it can predict likely sequences of text; for example
to respond to a message in a chat app.

00:01:07.074 --> 00:01:13.234
Users engage with the model through the chat app by
submitting prompts: natural language statements or

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:13.715
questions.

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:19.573
And the model responds by generating a sequence of
tokens to complete the conversational dialog.

00:01:21.373 --> 00:01:27.372
Generative AI solutions like this enable us to build
intelligent AI agents that can conduct conversations

00:01:27.372 --> 00:01:30.080
and even perform actions on our behalf.

00:01:31.281 --> 00:01:36.691
An agent, is an AI application that can operate autonomously
by using three key things.

00:01:36.941 --> 00:01:43.421
First, a large language model, which acts as the agent's
brain; enabling it to understand language and reason

00:01:43.421 --> 00:01:47.413
about how to respond to user requests or environmental
conditions.

00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:52.353
Second, an agent has instructions to scope its behavior
and responsibilities.

00:01:52.503 --> 00:01:55.185
Think of this as the agent's job description.

00:01:55.435 --> 00:02:01.915
And finally, most agents have tools; which enable
them to access the information they need, or use external

00:02:01.915 --> 00:02:03.400
systems to perform tasks.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:09.666
Users can engage with agents through prompts, for
example to get help with finding a particular product.

00:02:09.916 --> 00:02:15.676
The agent can use its tools to perform tasks like
searching the web, and placing an order for the required

00:02:15.676 --> 00:02:16.104
item.

00:02:16.354 --> 00:02:18.769
And finally, report back to the user.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:27.169
In the computing history application, I can see the
agent settings, including a model, instructions, and

00:02:27.169 --> 00:02:29.191
a tool that enables it to search the web.

00:02:29.441 --> 00:02:36.801
So I can task the agent with sourcing some parts that
I need for a PC restoration project, and it will search

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:38.334
for what I need on the web.

00:02:41.084 --> 00:02:46.251
And now that the agent has done the legwork for me,
I can go ahead and buy the part I need - sweet!

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:53.880
Generative AI apps and agents are at the heart of
modern AI solutions and are driving the development

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.653
of amazing new AI-powered capabilities.